"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9393],{69393:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"dorozhnye-stroiteli-prosrochka-kredity-oktyabr-2026","locale":"en","title":"Major road builders\' overdue loans have more than doubled since March to almost 9 billion rubles","description":"Overdue loans at major road builders have doubled to almost 9 billion rubles. How fewer orders and cash-flow gaps are hurting contractors.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"The National Association of Infrastructure Companies calculated the debts of 22 major road and bridge builders, including DSK Avtoban, Natsproektstroy and Roads and Bridges. By the end of August, their overdue loans had more than doubled from March to almost 9 billion rubles. Total debt is also rising: bank loans increased by 11.8% to 191 billion rubles, and amounts owed to suppliers and subcontractors rose by 16% to 495 billion. Two companies in the sample are already undergoing bankruptcy proceedings."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"Road construction is commissioned mainly by the state, and orders have declined. In January–September, regions spent about 1 trillion rubles on infrastructure construction, 19.1% less than a year earlier. The federal national projects Infrastructure for Life and Efficient Transport System received 886.8 billion, an increase of 3.1%, but this did not offset the regional decline. Clients unilaterally terminated 18 contracts worth 80.5 billion rubles with association members."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"Imagine a tradesperson renovating an apartment. They immediately pay for tiles, paint and an assistant\'s wages, but the owner pays only after accepting the work. If the owner delays payment, the tradesperson buys materials for the next job using a credit card. The longer the delay, the more profit goes to the bank as interest. This is a cash-flow gap. Road contractors bridge it with loans while the key rate is 14%, and according to Pavel Yakimchuk of CMWP, their actual borrowing costs are considerably higher."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"Banks recognize this risk and increasingly reject companies in the industry, even those with clean credit histories. Losing a bank guarantee is another danger: without it, a contractor must tie up its own money as security for a government contract or avoid new tenders. Association director Maria Yarmalchuk calls it a chain reaction. A client terminates a contract, a bank sees the risk and rejects other companies as well. In April the association warned of a wave of bankruptcies within 1–2 years; it says that forecast began to materialize over the following half-year."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"The budget lies behind the construction crisis. The federal budget deficit reached 5.2 trillion rubles over 9 months, and the annual target has been raised to 7.3 trillion. Regions are also cutting spending, with construction and repairs first to go because public-sector wages and social payments are harder to reduce. An increase in infrastructure national-project spending planned for 2027 has been postponed to 2028. Nationwide construction work declined by 2.9% in January–August."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"For regional residents, this means road repairs and construction may take longer; industry workers face a greater risk of delayed wages and layoffs. Yakimchuk expects overdue debt to keep rising for another 6–9 months. If spending increases in 2028 as planned, surviving contractors will receive work. But the longer the gap lasts, the fewer companies will survive to that point and the more concentrated the road-building market will become."},{"id":"b007","kind":"heading","text":"Why are road builders\' debts to banks rising?"},{"id":"b008","kind":"paragraph","text":"Over 9 months of 2026, regions cut infrastructure construction spending by 19.1%, clients terminated 18 contracts worth 80.5 billion rubles, and payment for ongoing contracts has been delayed. Contractors cover the gaps with loans, which become increasingly difficult to service at rates of 14% or more."},{"id":"b009","kind":"heading","text":"What is a cash-flow gap?"},{"id":"b010","kind":"paragraph","text":"It is a situation in which bills must be paid now but payment for completed work will arrive later. A company can be profitable on paper and still lack money for wages and materials. Borrowing covers the gap, and loan interest reduces profit."},{"id":"b011","kind":"heading","text":"How does the road-building crisis affect ordinary people?"},{"id":"b012","kind":"paragraph","text":"Road repairs and construction in the regions may take longer, and jobs in the industry are at risk. The increase in infrastructure national-project spending planned for 2027 has been postponed to 2028."}],"sources":[{"label":"Road builders become mired in debt","url":"https://www.kommersant.ru/doc/9044152"},{"label":"Infrastructure builders\' overdue debt doubles","url":"https://www.kommersant.ru/doc/9044417"},{"label":"Buried in debt","url":"https://lenta.ru/news/2026/10/22/pogryazli-v-dolgah"},{"label":"Russia\'s budget deficit narrows to 5.2 trillion rubles","url":"https://ru.themoscowtimes.com/2026/10/09/defitsit-byudzheta-rf-umenshilsya-do-52-trln-r-22-vvp-a208256"},{"label":"Two-thirds of regions will cut construction and improvement spending","url":"https://ru.themoscowtimes.com/2026/10/03/dve-treti-rossiiskih-regionov-urezhut-rashodi-na-stroitelstvo-i-blagoustroistvo-iz-za-defitsita-byudzheta-a207702"}],"sourceBodySha256":"6a480ee0961bbb53dd585c0158613bf1c2f51eef98dcb29ae725c420947d9032"}')}}]);